  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Thursday...\n\nPatchy fog may develop across the river valleys mainly around and\neast of Billings this morning. The Miles City area is shown to be\nthe most likely to see fog development. Use caution when \ncommuting this morning as fog may result in brief reductions to \nvisibility.\n\nAn active weather pattern arrives today and will last through the\nweekend. A weak cold front passed through the area early this \nmorning, as the influence of a trough moves through the Rockies. A\nsurface low in Wyoming will move eastward into the Great Plains \nahead of an advancing cold front associated with an upper trough \ndropping into Montana from Canada.\n\nSnow showers are expected to continue over the southwest portions\nof the Absaroka/Beartooth Mountains this morning. Meanwhile, the \nlatest Hi-Res models depict showers developing across portions of \nsouthern Montana and northern Wyoming with the moving surface low,\nby late morning. The latest deterministic GFS and ECMWF runs \nstill disagree with the Hi-Res models to an extent, instead \nshowing precipitation largely remaining in the mountains until \nlater this afternoon and evening with the secondary front and \ntrough.\n\nAll models do agree that as the upper trough along the Hi-Line \nwill tap into plenty of available moisture brought into the region\nby the preceding trough. Lower elevation snowfall is likely, with\nthe probability for at least 1 inch between 25 and 80 percent \nthrough Saturday morning. The probability being greatest as you go\nsouthwest of a line from Alzada to Melstone. The greatest \nprobability for at least 1 inch of snowfall exists along the \nMontana-Wyoming border. In the mountains, northwest aspects will \nbe favored tonight through Saturday. Several inches of \naccumulations are forecast with up to 6 inches possible at the \nhighest elevations of the Absaroka/Beartooths, Crazy, and Bighorn\nMountains. Prepare for wintery conditions if you have plans in \nthe backcountry this weekend.\n\nLocations most likely to see impacts from snowfall include the \nBeartooth/Red Lodge Foothills and Sheridan County, Wyoming. For\nthe Beartooth/Red Lodge Foothills, cross sections are showing a \nfavorable period of upslope flow beginning this afternoon and \nlasting into Saturday morning. The latest forecast has 1 to 4\ninches of snowfall in Red Lodge with locally higher amounts\npossible. The latest 90th percentile from the REFS shows up to 6 \ninches for Red Lodge. A Winter Weather Advisory will go into \neffect at 11 AM MST today and go until 11 AM MST Saturday. Use \ncaution when traveling near the foothills.\n\nSheridan will be tricky as local terrain effects could bring \nlocally greater snowfall with upslope flow. Or if the flow is too \nstrong, downsloping would mitigate the extent of total \nprecipitation amounts. This will continue to be monitored today.\n\nSteep lapse rates are in place as the cold front passes through, \nthis will allow for higher snowfall rates across the plains where \nany showers develop. A few heavy showers are depicted over far \nsoutheast Montana and northern Wyoming. The probability for \nsnowfall rates of 1\"/hour is low, around 10% with the heaviest \nshowers at this time. Overall, forecast precipitation amounts \nrange from a trace up to a few tenths of an inch. The greatest \nchance for more than 0.1\" of precipitation exists along US-212 to \nI-90 from Alzada to Billings to Livingston and south.\n\nThe chance for precipitation will diminish through Saturday, \nleaving only showers over the southwest mountains into Sunday. \nBelow normal temperatures are forecast through Monday, with highs \nin the 30sF. Temperatures will begin to warm into the 40sF, \nTuesday through late next week. The chance for precipitation is \ncurrently low outside of occasional mountain showers.\n\nMatos",
